          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Differential characters
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#CheegerSimons85"}> Cheeger &amp; Simons 1985
            \&amp;rbrack; are one geometric model for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            = succeed "-refinement" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            – i.e. of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} represented
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} Eilenberg-MacLane spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(-,\mathbb{Z})
          %p
            Accordingly, Cheeger-Simons differential characters model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundles+with+connection"} circle n-bundles with connection
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            \-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            \-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbes"}> bundle gerbes
            ) and as such are equivalent to other models for these structures, notably to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
            these are equivalently ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            on ordinary
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
          %p
            The definition of CS-differential characters encodes rather directly the higher dimensional notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            of such higher connections: a Cheeger-Simons-character is a rule that assigns values in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
